{% doc %}
  A group: a flex container that holds other blocks and carries its own size,
  background, border and link.

  ## Alignment always means the same thing

  The hardest part of a layout control is that "Left" has to mean left whether
  the group stacks or sits side by side — but in CSS it is `align-items` in one
  case and `justify-content` in the other. Exposing the CSS properties directly
  is why so many builders have an Alignment control that moves things vertically
  half the time.

  So the merchant gets two controls with fixed meanings:

    Alignment  always the horizontal axis
    Position   always the vertical axis

  and this snippet writes them to two custom properties, `--group-inline` and
  `--group-block`. `base.css` then decides which CSS property reads which, based
  on direction. One place knows about the swap; the merchant never does.

  ## Distribution is separate from alignment

  "Space between" is not an alignment — it is what happens to the *gaps*, and it
  only exists on the main axis. Rather than adding a fourth option to Alignment
  that silently does nothing when the group is stacked, it is its own control,
  shown only when the group is horizontal. No option in this block is ever
  inert.

  ## Width, and why Fill is not 100%

  Inside a horizontal parent, "Fill" has to mean *take the space that is left*,
  which is `flex: 1`, not `width: 100%` — the latter overflows as soon as there
  is a sibling. Fit is `flex: 0 0 auto`, Custom is `flex: 0 0 <n>%`. All three
  behave correctly in a stacked parent too, where there is no space to compete
  for.

  Mobile takes its own width because the desktop answer is usually wrong on a
  phone: two 50% columns want to be one 100% column, and that is a different
  value rather than a scale of the same one.

  ## The link

  A group that is a link cannot be an `<a>`: it contains blocks, and blocks
  contain buttons and links of their own — an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid
  and browsers recover from it unpredictably.

  Instead the group stays a `<div>` and an absolutely positioned `<a>` covers
  it. Anything genuinely interactive inside raises itself above that overlay in
  `base.css`, so a button inside a linked group is still a button and the rest
  of the surface is still the link.

  Parameters:
    settings           (object, required) Block settings
    children           (string)  Rendered child blocks
    shopify_attributes (string)  Editor attributes

  @example
    {% render 'group', children: children, settings: block.settings,
       shopify_attributes: block.shopify_attributes %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign direction = settings.direction | default: 'column'
  assign is_row = false
  if direction == 'row'
    assign is_row = true
  endif

  # ---- axes -------------------------------------------------------------
  # Written as flexbox keywords here so the stylesheet does not need a lookup
  # table of its own. `start` and `end` rather than `flex-start`: the modern
  # keywords work for both `align-items` and `justify-content`, which is what
  # lets one property serve both axes.
  case settings.alignment
    when 'center'
      assign inline_axis = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign inline_axis = 'end'
    else
      assign inline_axis = 'start'
  endcase

  case settings.position
    when 'center'
      assign block_axis = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign block_axis = 'end'
    else
      assign block_axis = 'start'
  endcase

  # Distribution replaces the inline alignment, because both land on
  # `justify-content` and the later one would win arbitrarily. Only meaningful
  # on a row, so ignored entirely on a column.
  #
  # Coerced with `default` and compared against a real string rather than tested
  # with `!= blank`. An unset setting is nil in Shopify and Undefined in other
  # Liquid engines, and only one of those is blank — a difference that silently
  # emitted `--group-inline:` with no value.
  assign distribution = settings.distribution | default: 'none'
  if is_row and distribution != 'none'
    assign inline_axis = distribution
  endif

  # ---- size -------------------------------------------------------------
  assign width_pct = settings.width_custom | default: 100 | plus: 0
  assign width_mobile_pct = settings.width_custom_mobile | default: 100 | plus: 0

  # Two properties, not one.
  #
  # `flex` only means anything to a flex *item*, and a group is only a flex item
  # when its parent is another group. At the top level of a section — inside
  # `.rich-text__inner`, a `.container`, anywhere that is not `display: flex` —
  # a `flex` declaration is inert, so Fit and Custom did nothing and every group
  # rendered full width. That is the bug.
  #
  # So each option also writes an `inline-size`. Inside a flex row the
  # `flex-basis` wins for the initial main size and the width is ignored; outside
  # one the width is all there is. Both agree, so neither context is a special
  # case.
  case settings.width
    when 'fill'
      assign width_value = '1 1 0%'
      assign width_size = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign width_value = '0 0 ' | append: width_pct | append: '%'
      assign width_size = width_pct | append: '%'
    else
      assign width_value = '0 0 auto'
      assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  endcase

  case settings.width_mobile
    when 'fill'
      assign width_mobile_value = '1 1 0%'
      assign width_mobile_size = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign width_mobile_value = '0 0 ' | append: width_mobile_pct | append: '%'
      assign width_mobile_size = width_mobile_pct | append: '%'
    else
      assign width_mobile_value = '0 0 auto'
      assign width_mobile_size = 'fit-content'
  endcase

  case settings.height
    when 'fill'
      assign height_value = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign custom_height = settings.height_custom | default: 300 | plus: 0
      assign height_value = custom_height | append: 'px'
    else
      assign height_value = 'auto'
  endcase

  # ---- border -----------------------------------------------------------
  assign border_width = 0
  if settings.border_style == 'solid'
    assign border_width = settings.border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
  endif

  assign radius = settings.border_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0

  # ---- flags ------------------------------------------------------------
  assign background_media = settings.background_media | default: 'none'
  assign has_media = false
  if background_media != 'none'
    assign has_media = true
  endif

  assign link_url = settings.link | default: ''
  assign has_link = false
  if link_url != ''
    assign has_link = true
  endif

  # ---- classes ----------------------------------------------------------
  assign classes = 'block-group'
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--' | append: direction
  if settings.stack_on_mobile and is_row
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--stack-mobile'
  endif
  if has_media
    assign classes = classes | append: ' has-background-media'
  endif
  if has_link
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--linked'
  endif
  assign scheme = settings.color_scheme | default: ''
  if scheme != ''
    assign classes = classes | append: ' color-' | append: scheme
  endif
-%}

{%- capture group_style -%}
  --group-gap: {{ settings.gap | default: 16 }}px;
  --group-inline: {{ inline_axis }};
  --group-block: {{ block_axis }};
  --group-width: {{ width_value }};
  --group-width-size: {{ width_size }};
  --group-width-mobile: {{ width_mobile_value }};
  --group-width-mobile-size: {{ width_mobile_size }};
  --group-height: {{ height_value }};
  --group-radius: {{ radius }}px;
  {%- if border_width > 0 -%}
    --group-border-width: {{ border_width }}px;
    --group-border-color: {{ settings.border_color | default: 'currentColor' }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign background_color = settings.background_color | default: '' -%}
  {%- if background_color != '' and has_media == false -%}
    --group-background: {{ background_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  style="{{ group_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {% render 'spacing', block: settings %}
  {{ shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if has_media -%}
    {% render 'background-media', settings: settings %}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The children sit in their own layer so they stay above the background media
    and above the link overlay. Without it a group with a background would put
    its own backdrop over its contents.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="block-group__content">
    {{- children -}}
  </div>

  {%- if has_link -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `aria-label` falls back to the URL only if the merchant left it blank,
      which is worse than a real label but far better than a link a screen
      reader announces as nothing at all.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a
      class="block-group__link"
      href="{{ link_url }}"
      {% if settings.link_new_tab %}target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ settings.link_label | default: link_url | escape }}"
    ></a>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
